@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Subqueries Introduction";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Subqueries Introduction
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how to use a query inside another SQL query
            to retrieve and analyze related data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS A SUBQUERY?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Subquery?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Subquery</strong> is a query written inside
                another SQL query. The inner query is called the
                <strong>subquery</strong>, and the outer query is called
                the <strong>main query</strong>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Subqueries are useful when the result of one query is
                required as input for another query.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC STRUCTURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Structure of a Subquery
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name = (
    SELECT column_name
    FROM another_table
    WHERE condition
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3 mb-0">
                The inner query executes first and its result is then
                used by the outer query.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple Subquery Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we have an <strong>Employees</strong> table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border p-3 rounded"><code>
EmployeeId    EmployeeName    Salary
-----------   -------------   -------
1             Rahul           30000
2             Priya           45000
3             Amit            50000
4             Sneha           40000
            </code></pre>

            <p>
                Now suppose we want to find employees whose salary is
                greater than the average salary.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; (
    SELECT AVG(Salary)
    FROM Employees
);
            </code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                How it works
            </h5>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    The inner query calculates the average salary.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The outer query compares each employee's salary
                    with that average.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Employees whose salary is greater than the average
                    are returned.
                </li>
            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUBQUERY WITH WHERE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Subquery with WHERE
            </h2>

            <p>
                A subquery is commonly used with the
                <strong>WHERE</strong> clause to filter records based
                on the result of another query.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; (
    SELECT Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE EmployeeName = 'Rahul'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                In this example, the inner query finds Rahul's salary.
                The outer query then returns employees whose salary is
                greater than Rahul's salary.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUBQUERY WITH IN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Subquery with IN
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>IN</strong> operator can be used when a
                subquery returns multiple values.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName
FROM Employees
WHERE DepartmentId IN (
    SELECT DepartmentId
    FROM Departments
    WHERE Location = 'Pune'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query returns department IDs for departments
                located in Pune. The outer query then returns employees
                belonging to those departments.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TYPES OF SUBQUERIES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Types of Subqueries
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Type</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Single-Row Subquery</td>
                            <td>
                                Returns a single value or single row.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Multiple-Row Subquery</td>
                            <td>
                                Returns multiple rows or values.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Correlated Subquery</td>
                            <td>
                                Depends on a value from the outer query.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Subquery with DML</td>
                            <td>
                                Subqueries can be used with
                                SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A subquery is enclosed within parentheses.
            </li>

            <li>
                The inner query is written inside the outer query.
            </li>

            <li>
                A subquery can be used with WHERE, HAVING, FROM and
                other SQL clauses.
            </li>

            <li>
                The type of operator used depends on whether the
                subquery returns one value or multiple values.
            </li>

            <li>
                Subqueries are useful for solving complex SQL problems.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            A subquery is a query inside another SQL query. It allows
            the result of one query to be used by another query.
            Subqueries are commonly used with WHERE and IN and are
            useful for filtering and analyzing related data.
        </p>

    </div>


